<TopicPage slug="subjects/chemistry/expert/quantum-chemistry">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Quantum chemistry is where chemistry's rules are finally derived instead of memorised. Why
    shells hold 2, 8, 8; why carbon makes four bonds in a tetrahedron; why the periodic table's
    rows have the lengths they do — all of it falls out of <strong>solving the Schrödinger
    equation for electrons around nuclei</strong>. The exact solutions exist only for hydrogen;
    the rest of the field is clever approximation, now supercharged by computation.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="from wave equation to periodic table — the logical chain" widget="equationStep" config={orbitalCfg} />

  <h2>What the theory delivers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Molecular orbital theory:</strong> atomic orbitals combine into bonding and antibonding molecular orbitals. Bond order (bonding minus antibonding, halved) predicts stability — and explains why O₂ is paramagnetic (two lonely unpaired electrons), a fact Lewis structures can't see.</li>
    <li><strong>Computational chemistry:</strong> density functional theory (DFT, Nobel 1998) approximates the electron density instead of the full wavefunction — good enough to screen battery materials, catalysts and drug candidates before anyone touches a lab bench.</li>
    <li><strong>Spectroscopy explained:</strong> every spectral line is a quantum jump between solved energy levels — the theory and the measurements close to many decimal places.</li>
    <li><strong>Still hard:</strong> exact solutions scale brutally with electron count. The frontier — quantum computers simulating quantum chemistry (Feynman's 1982 suggestion) — exists precisely because the equations are so demanding.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Schrödinger (1926); Pauling, The Nature of the Chemical Bond (1939); Pople & Kohn, Nobel lectures (1998).</p>
</TopicPage>
